<h2>Where the news comes from</h2>
<p>Every day a program gathers what is new from public sources: PubMed, Europe PMC, bioRxiv and medRxiv preprints, the Nature, Science and Cell RSS feeds (title, authors, date and link only) and headlines from SINC, The Conversation and ScienceDaily. Nobody hand-picks what goes into «Latest» or «Nature · Science · Cell». «Classics» are the exception: the site proposes them and only those Sofía approves are published.</p>

<h2>The three signals of every study</h2>
<p>Always visible, on the card and on the study page. When a piece of data does not exist the signal says «no data»: we never guess.</p>
<dl class="method-signals">
  <dt>Review</dt>
  <dd><strong>Preprint</strong>: it comes from bioRxiv or medRxiv and has not been peer reviewed yet. <strong>Published in a journal</strong>: it appears in a journal. Note that this alone does not prove it was peer reviewed. <span class="gap"><strong>Gap</strong> Sofía decides the final name of this signal.</span></dd>
  <dt>Subjects</dt>
  <dd><strong>Humans</strong>, <strong>animals</strong> or both, from the MeSH descriptors added by the US National Library of Medicine. Newly published studies do not have them yet, and then the signal says «no data».</dd>
  <dt>Notices</dt>
  <dd><strong>Retracted</strong>, <strong>corrected</strong> or <strong>expression of concern</strong>, from the notices publishers deposit in Crossref. We check that list every day, so a retraction that arrives years later shows up too.</dd>
</dl>

<h2>How summaries are written</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Short facts are taken from the article's abstract, and each fact must be in the original.</li>
  <li>A language model writes the English text from those facts only, then the Spanish text.</li>
  <li>Automatic checks: every sentence cites its source; figures and names match it; never 8 or more words in a row copied; no overclaiming («cure», «proves that», «breakthrough»…); the study's caveats are kept; and the Spanish spelling is correct.</li>
  <li>A judge, a model from another family, rereads each summary. Without its approval nothing is published.</li>
  <li>If anything fails, the summary is not published: a <strong>fallback text</strong> built only from the data is shown instead (for example: «Study in animals published in Neuron on 5 November 2026»).</li>
</ol>
<p>We never publish abstracts or paragraphs of the articles: only our own summaries, with a link to the original and its licence.</p>

<h2>What we do not do</h2>
<p>SYNAPTICA is science communication. It gives no medical advice and recommends no treatments.</p>
